 {'h2':'HealthRX evidence card: what the archive supports','paragraphs':["The HealthRX source card supports a narrow list of commercial statements: a starting figure for compounded semaglutide, a separate starting figure for compounded tirzepatide, no long-term commitment, provider review, ongoing clinical guidance, HSA/FSA use, financing, insurance generally not covering compounded medication, licensed U.S. compounding pharmacy language, approval within two days, and a seven-to-ten-day shipping statement. Each statement stays attached to the captured page and retrieval date. The card does not support a guaranteed prescription, a named personal clinician for every state, a universal final price, an outcome, or an assurance that a pharmacy shipment will follow the same timeline.","That distinction determines the prose. We can say the page describes ongoing guidance; we cannot say a particular person receives a specific number of visits. We can cite licensed U.S. pharmacy language; we cannot convert it into proof about a future prescription. We can compare advertised starting figures; we cannot calculate an individual course. The evidence card is therefore both a source and a boundary around the source." ]},
 {'h2':'AgelessRx evidence card: the plan-format advantage','paragraphs':["The AgelessRx source card supports compounded semaglutide from $150 monthly, compounded tirzepatide from $250 monthly, no membership fee, no contract, no obligation, optional three- and six-month formats with savings, HSA/FSA payment, physician review, ongoing support, accredited compounding pharmacy language, included shipping, and a four-to-six-business-day post-processing delivery statement. Those details make commitment cadence and fulfillment more visible than a page containing only a headline amount.","The card still leaves boundaries. “Savings” does not quantify the final amount for every product. “No obligation” does not state every renewal cutoff or refund condition. “Physician review” does not prove that a prescription will be issued. “After processing” does not define the time before processing completes. The comparison preserves those gaps instead of smoothing them into a winner claim." ]},
 {'h2':'How a self-pay shopper can compare without choosing medically','paragraphs':["A nonclinical decision worksheet can compare the shape of the transaction: entry price, recurring price, plan length, membership charge, consultation inclusion, support description, payment methods, pharmacy identification, review milestone, fulfillment milestone, cancellation cutoff, and source date. Those questions are legitimate editorial comparison fields because they describe a service contract and evidence trail. They do not ask a publisher to select a medication or predict a response.","If HealthRX supplies the stronger written all-in quote, its higher disclosure score may align with the shopper’s administrative priority. If AgelessRx supplies clearer multi-month terms and the shopper values plan flexibility, that evidence may matter more. Neither outcome belongs in a medical verdict. The worksheet ends with a provider conversation, not a prescription recommendation." ]},
 {'h2':'Freshness and correction protocol','paragraphs':["Prices and policy language are volatile. Recheck both official pages before indexing, after a material provider-page revision, and on the editorial refresh date. Store the new quote beside the old quote rather than silently replacing history. If a price disappears, mark it unknown. If a provider clarifies a cancellation term, archive the dated clarification and update only the affected field.","A provider or reader correction should identify the page, disputed sentence, supporting first-party source, and date. The editorial desk can then reproduce the claim and publish a correction note. This process is slower than copying a landing page but protects the comparison from stale numbers and undocumented reversals." ]}
