 {'h2':'Read promotional arithmetic without projecting a treatment course','paragraphs':["Wellorithm’s month-one and later-month display allows a reader to see that the entry amount is not the recurring amount. Enhance.MD’s captured monthly display does not show the same promotional step-up. It would still be misleading to multiply either figure into a personal treatment total because the prescribed product, route, duration, later price changes, optional services, and eligibility are unresolved. The correct editorial move is to label the billing period and request a quote, not extrapolate a clinical course.","A comparison table can sort the published entry number while the prose warns that numeric order is not total-cost order. That is why the score includes price transparency rather than raw cheapness. A provider earns transparency credit for explaining the bill, not merely for displaying the smallest numeral." ]},
 {'h2':'Payment tools solve cash flow, not evidence gaps','paragraphs':["Both captured pages discuss HSA/FSA use, while Wellorithm also mentions financing and Enhance.MD says insurance is not accepted. An HSA/FSA statement concerns a payment instrument. Financing concerns timing of payment. Insurance acceptance concerns claim processing. These are different fields, and none proves that a particular expense will be reimbursed or eligible under a person’s plan.","Before purchase, a shopper can ask for the merchant descriptor, itemized receipt, financing cost, refund treatment, and documentation supplied for an HSA/FSA administrator. Clinic Scout should not promise tax treatment or benefit eligibility. The comparison records what the provider says and leaves personal plan interpretation to the plan administrator or qualified adviser." ]},
 {'h2':'Temperature language needs an operational follow-up','paragraphs':["Wellorithm’s refrigerated-shipping statement is more specific than generic free-shipping copy, but it does not answer every operational question. Ask how the package is insulated, which temperature instructions accompany the exact product, how tracking is delivered, what to do after a delay, and which organization handles replacement. Enhance.MD’s delivery range likewise needs a clear trigger and exception policy.","These questions matter because a carrier estimate is not a quality review. Clinic Scout does not inspect packages or validate storage conditions. It can only distinguish a provider that publishes logistics detail from one that leaves it unstated, then direct the reader to obtain product-specific instructions from the dispensing pharmacy." ]},
 {'h2':'A renewal audit prevents accidental apples-to-oranges ranking','paragraphs':["On the day before checkout, write down the advertised product, route, first charge, second charge, renewal frequency, cancellation channel, cutoff, and pharmacy-processing point. Repeat the audit when the provider changes the prescribed option or invoice. This catches the moment when a promotional figure becomes a recurring figure or a different form carries a different amount.","For Wellorithm, the central audit question is the transition from month one to later months. For Enhance.MD, it is whether the compact monthly figure remains complete after review. Those are genuinely different comparison jobs, which is why this article does not reuse the HealthRX-versus-AgelessRx commitment framework." ]},
 {'h2':'Source-change handling for this pair','paragraphs':["The Wellorithm and Enhance.MD captures are dated snapshots. A later landing page may change the entry amount, remove a route, add a fee, or revise timing. Refresh the exact quote and re-run the scoring rubric when a material field changes. Do not preserve an old winner label after the evidence underneath it moves.","If a page becomes inaccessible, retain the archived evidence for provenance but mark the active field unverified. A commercial comparison should degrade gracefully into unknowns rather than rely on an expired claim. Publication and live-link gates remain separate even when the copy is current." ]}
